{
  "image_name": "4a890e8bb715e39a52f3e9ec39cad711.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes iron handling and redox pathways that control ferroptosis, highlighting factors that either promote or suppress this form of cell death. Iron enters cells mainly through transferrin receptor uptake and other import routes, is stored in ferritin, exported by SLC40A1, or directed to mitochondria and heme-related processes. Ferritin breakdown via ferritinophagy appears to release additional iron, increasing the labile Fe²⁺ pool. This iron fuels reactive oxygen species production through Fenton chemistry and related oxidase systems, leading to lipid peroxide formation.\n\nA central theme is the balance between pro-ferroptotic lipid oxidation and anti-ferroptotic antioxidant defense. On the lipid side, enzymes such as ACSL4, LPCAT3, and ALOXs channel polyunsaturated fatty acids into membrane phospholipids and oxidize them into toxic lipid hydroperoxides. On the protective side, the system xc⁻ transporter imports cystine for glutathione synthesis, and GPX4 uses glutathione to detoxify lipid peroxides. Mitochondrial iron-sulfur cluster and citrate-related proteins also appear linked to iron regulation.\n\nThe color coding reinforces the overall message: blue components generally drive ferroptosis by increasing iron availability or lipid oxidation, whereas orange components counter it by exporting iron, storing it, supporting glutathione metabolism, or removing lipid peroxides.",
